      "source_text": "A small environmental archive began digitizing field notebooks created by survey teams between 1987 and 2004. The notebooks contained handwritten observations, sketches, pasted labels and occasional measurements written in pencil. Because the originals were unique. the project was designed around preservation first and speed second Every notebook received a temporary work number, and staff photographed its cover and condition before scanning. Pages with loose inserts were flagged so that no item would be lost when the binding was opened. The scanning station used an overhead camera rather than a flatbed scanner because several bindings could not be pressed safely. Pages were supported at a gentle angle and illuminaled by two diffuse lamps positioned to reduce glare. A grey reference card appeared in the first image of each session. Most pages were captured at 400 ppi equivalent resolution, while maps and pages containing very small annotations were recorded at higher resolution. Operators checked focus at the centre and corners before continuing through a notebook. File naming was deliberately simple. A notebook identified as FN-1994-17 produced images such as FN-1994-17_P001 and FN-1994-17_P002. If a loose insert belonged between pages 18 and",
